================
DHCP Snooping Rate Limiting

Why we did this
================
DHCP Snooping protects the network from Rogue DHCP Servers.

However, attackers found another way to attack DHCP services.

They started sending thousands of DHCP Discover messages to the DHCP Server.

This attack is called:

DHCP Starvation Attack

The goal is to exhaust all available IP addresses in the DHCP pool.

Once the DHCP pool becomes empty:

- Legitimate users cannot obtain IP addresses.
- Network connectivity is disrupted.
- Attackers may introduce a Rogue DHCP Server.

To prevent excessive DHCP requests, Cisco introduced DHCP Snooping Rate Limiting.

What will happen
================
1. Administrator sets a maximum number of DHCP packets allowed per second.
2. The switch monitors DHCP traffic on untrusted ports.
3. If DHCP packets exceed the configured threshold:
   - Packets are dropped.
   - Security violation occurs.
   - Port may be error-disabled.
4. DHCP Starvation attacks are prevented.

Example

Without Rate Limiting
---------------------

Attacker PC
      |
      |
Thousands of DHCP Requests
      |
      |
Switch ------ DHCP Server

Result:
--------
DHCP Pool Exhausted


With Rate Limiting
------------------

Attacker PC
      |
      |
Hundreds of DHCP Requests
      |
      |
Switch

Configured Limit:
15 Packets Per Second

Result:
--------
Excess Packets Dropped
DHCP Pool Protected

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================

Advantages
----------
1. Prevents DHCP Starvation attacks.
2. Protects DHCP address pools.
3. Prevents denial-of-service attacks against DHCP.
4. Improves network stability.
5. Works with DHCP Snooping.
6. Protects legitimate users.
7. Simple to configure.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. Incorrect limits may block valid traffic.
2. Different environments require different thresholds.
3. Additional monitoring is required.
4. Only protects DHCP traffic.
5. Does not stop Rogue DHCP Servers by itself (requires DHCP Snooping).

Hence what came next
--------------------
Even after protecting DHCP servers, attackers could still introduce unauthorized DHCP servers into the network.

Administrators needed a method to identify and block those rogue servers.

This led to:

Rogue DHCP Detection

which focuses on identifying unauthorized devices attempting to provide DHCP services.

One-Shot Configuration
======================

Enable DHCP Snooping

Switch(config)# ip dhcp snooping
Switch(config)# ip dhcp snooping vlan 10,20,30


Configure Trusted Port
(DHCP Server Port)

Switch(config)# interface g0/1
Switch(config-if)# ip dhcp snooping trust


Configure Rate Limit on User Ports

Switch(config)# interface fa0/1
Switch(config-if)# ip dhcp snooping limit rate 15

Switch(config)# interface fa0/2
Switch(config-if)# ip dhcp snooping limit rate 15

Switch(config)# interface fa0/3
Switch(config-if)# ip dhcp snooping limit rate 15

Verification
============

show ip dhcp snooping

show ip dhcp snooping statistics

show ip dhcp snooping binding

